{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cg6Ve2QVW2zMqC8oQF6kY", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "```\nTable 3 reports the estimates, and they point to a clear role for reviewer identity. In column 2, the coefficient on the identity-disclosed indicator is 0.18 (SE = 0.04). Adding product fixed effects in column 3 leaves the effect largely intact, at 0.15 (SE = 0.05). The identity effect also grows with reviewer credibility: the interaction with reviewer tenure in column 4 is 0.09 (SE = 0.03). Star rating remains a strong predictor, with a coefficient of 0.42 (SE = 0.02) across all specifications, yet it does not displace identity. Explanatory power rises from an R-squared of 0.31 to 0.47 across the columns. All coefficients are significant at the 1% level except the interaction, which is significant at the 5% level.\n```\n\nI kept every number, SE, and significance level as given. I added a few framing phrases (\"largely intact,\" \"grows with reviewer credibility,\" \"does not displace identity\") to give the paragraph direction. These go slightly beyond your original wording, so check that you're comfortable with them. In particular, \"grows with reviewer credibility\" treats tenure as a credibility proxy, and \"does not displace identity\" is an interpretive claim. Cut either if your paper doesn't make that argument elsewhere."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1881, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1496, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1881}, "output_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "19b6103d-cbfc-505f-8dc1-c40cc8379cf6", "uuid": "8b51758f-d1e3-408e-ad69-